<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transitional National Government</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2000">2000</a>–<a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Transitional National Government was an interim government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> formed in 2000 at a peace conference at Arta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a>, the first attempt at a national government since the state collapsed in 1991. Led by President Abdiqasim Salad Hassan, it controlled only parts of Mogadishu and was rejected by many faction leaders, and it gave way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transitional-federal-government">Transitional Federal Government</a> in 2004.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The government took shape at the Somalia National Peace Conference, held at Arta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a> from 20 April to 5 May 2000, where its name was chosen and clan representatives elected Abdiqasim Salad Hassan president. The Transitional National Charter behind it recognised de facto regional autonomy and the new entities in the north of the former Somalia. The Islamic Courts, whose militias had united into one force that year, backed the new government: they protected the president on his return to the capital in August 2000, handed over their heavy weapons, and were absorbed during 2001.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> opposed the government from the start, fearing renewed claims on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a>, and sponsored opposition alliances, above all the Somalia Reconciliation and Restoration Council, a warlord coalition founded in Ethiopia in mid-2001. Abdullahi Yusuf Ahmed, leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puntland">Puntland</a>, asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in January 2001 to review its recognition and joined the coalition. After the attacks of 11 September 2001 the Ethiopian government accused the government's leaders of Islamic extremism, and the council called for international intervention.</p><p>According to the scholar Le Sage, by 2002 the government had all the organs of a state, including a parliament, a police force and a standing army, which in March 2002 numbered 2,010 men and 90 women. Its institutions remained weak, lacking office equipment, territorial control and tax revenue, and it could not provide basic services; ministers complained of being shut out of decisions and of irregular pay. It had four prime ministers, beginning with Ali Khalif Galaydh, and was reported bankrupt in December 2003.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> backed the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transitional-federal-government">Transitional Federal Government</a> in 2004 and the presidency of Abdullahi Yusuf, expecting him to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>'s claim to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a>. On 10 October 2004 the new parliament elected Yusuf with 189 votes against 79 for Abdullahi Ahmed Addou in the third round, and Abdiqasim Salad Hassan withdrew peacefully. The academics Ashley Elliot and Georg-Sebastian Holzer concluded that Ethiopia's success in undermining the interim government showed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> held an effective veto over its neighbour's political direction.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Transitional National Government of Somalia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A <b>Transitional National Government</b> formed in 2000 and a Transitional Federal Government in 2004.</blockquote><a href="/state/somalia">Somalia</a></li></ul></section>
